thinking:
The decode_chunked handles "0" final chunk. The problem is the READ loop doesn't stop at the 0-chunk — it keeps calling mbedtls_ssl_read waiting for more data (keep-alive), blocking ~60s until the server closes.

The fix: in the body read loop (in tls_read_response), when `chunked`, stop reading once the accumulated raw buffer contains the final chunk terminator. The terminator in the raw stream is: "...\r\n0\r\n\r\n" (end of body). Detection: after each read (or at loop check), if chunked and the raw buffer ends with "0\r\n\r\n" preceded by "\r\n" (i.e., suffix "\r\n0\r\n\r\n") — or more precisely, contains a complete final chunk. The chunk data could theoretically contain "0\r\n\r\n" — for robustness, parse chunk structure. But for a pragmatic fix, suffix-check "\r\n0\r\n\r\n" at the buffer end is what actually occurs on the wire from real servers; the loop reads sequentially so we can check the accumulated suffix each iteration. Edge case: a chunk boundary split across reads — suffix check on the accumulated buffer handles that (the suffix grows with each read).

Also should handle: response body might already contain the full body + terminator in the initial raw fragment (the header read pulled in body bytes too — `initial` fragment). The current loop checks `content_length >= 0 && resp_pos >= content_length` for the break. Add: `if (chunked && ends_with_term(resp_buf, resp_pos)) break;` — and check BEFORE the loop too (in case the initial fragment already contains the complete terminator).

Implementation: add a small helper:

```c
/* Check whether buf[0..len) ends with the chunked final-chunk terminator:
 * "\r\n0\r\n\r\n" (final chunk) — the body may also end with trailers, but
 * real LLM backends (DeepSeek etc.) send "0\r\n\r\n" directly. */
static int chunked_term_seen(const char* buf, size_t len)
{
    static const char term[] = "\r\n0\r\n\r\n";
    size_t n = sizeof(term) - 1;
    if (len < n) return 0;
    return memcmp(buf + len - n, term, n) == 0;
